# URLs the link checker cannot verify, one regex per line.
#
# lychee reads this file automatically, so both `validate.yml` (changed
# files on a PR) and `linkcheck.yml` (the whole dataset, weekly) honour it.
#
# Every entry states why it is here and when that was observed. An
# exclusion without a reason is indistinguishable from hiding a broken
# link, and these are the URLs behind `primary_reference_url` values —
# the dataset's evidence trail (CLAUDE.md §5.2). Two kinds live here:
#
#   * DEAD    — genuinely broken, kept visible until someone confirms a
#               replacement. Excluded so one known failure does not mask
#               the next new one.
#   * REFUSES — alive in a browser, refuses an automated client. The
#               checker cannot speak for these; a human has to. Note that
#               `--user-agent` was tried first, honestly identifying the
#               checker and linking back to the repo (2026-09-07); these
#               three still refused, so the block is not about the UA
#               string.
#
# Re-check these by hand at each release and delete the line when it
# starts working. Nothing else belongs in this file.

# DEAD 2026-09-07 — HTTP 404. Backs the primary_reference_url family for
# every standards_body_wg row, so it needs a confirmed replacement rather
# than a guessed one. See sources/registry.yml → ieee_sa_ai.
^https://standards\.ieee\.org/practices/intelligent-systems/$

# REFUSES 2026-09-07 — HTTP 401 to any automated client.
^https://mlcommons\.org/working-groups/ai-safety/

# REFUSES 2026-09-07 — closes the connection with an HTTP/2 protocol
# error; answered 403 before the user agent was set, so the refusal is
# the point, not the protocol.
^https://www\.g20\.org/

# REFUSES 2026-09-07 — HTTP 500 to the checker. The same feed is fetched
# successfully by pipelines/fetchers/tech_policy_press.py, so the
# resource is plainly alive; the 500 is the WAF's answer, not the site's.
^https://www\.techpolicy\.press/category/artificial-intelligence/feed/$

# Not a URL: the classifier's synthetic provenance scheme for
# LLM-classified fields (CLAUDE.md §4.4).
^internal://
